import {
  event, table1, munitions, monthly, inflation, admin, ig, computed,
  costBars, clocksRows, timeline, fuel, battleLosses, flyingHours,
} from "../../../data/iran-war-2026-09-15-cbo-estimates-38-billion-war-cost.mjs";
const state = readPublishedState().events.get("event-iran-war-2026-09-15-cbo-estimates-38-billion-war-cost");
if (!state) throw new Error("Missing publication selection: event-iran-war-2026-09-15-cbo-estimates-38-billion-war-cost");
if (state.status === "merged") return Astro.redirect(eventPath(state.survivor), 301);
---
{state.status === "retired" ? <StateEvent view={state} /> : <BaseLayout title={`${event.title} | The Catch`} current="Events">
  <div slot="locator" class="locator"><a href="/events/">Series</a><a href="/events/iran-war/">Iran war cost</a><span>September 15, 2026</span></div>
  <div class="content">
    <main class="story" id="story-root">
      <p class="story-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Congressional Budget Office &middot; September 15, 2026</p>
      <h1 data-layer="fact">{event.title}</h1>
      <p class="story-dek" data-layer="fact">{event.dek}</p>
      <ReadingModes />

      <nav class="story-toc" aria-label="In this story" data-layer="fact">
        <details class="toc-disclosure" open><summary><span>In this story</span><span class="toc-current" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#what-happened">What happened</a></li>
          <li><a href="#the-catch">The catch</a></li>
          <li><a href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-feels-it">Who feels it</a></li>
          <li><a href="#outlets">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</a></li>
          <li><a href="#claim-check">Claim checked against the record</a></li>
          <li><a href="#what-happened-next">What happened next</a></li>
          <li><a href="#unknowns">What we do not know yet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#records">The records</a></li>
        </ol>
      </details></nav>
      <div class="kpi-strip" data-layer="fact">
        {event.kpis.map((k) => (
          <div class="kpi">
            <span class="kpi-value">{k.value}<small>{k.unit ? ` ${k.unit}` : ""}</small></span>
            <span class="kpi-label">{k.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <aside class="three-things" data-layer="fact">
        <p class="tt-label">Three things to know</p>
        <ol>
          <li>On September 15 the Congressional Budget Office sent Ranking Member Brendan Boyle a 19-page letter. Table 1 says combat operations against Iran have cost the Department of Defense ${table1.total} billion through August 1, in 2026 dollars. The opening summary rounds that same total to about $38 billion.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /></li>
          <li>Replacing expended munitions is ${munitions.total} billion of that total, more than half. Extra flying hours are ${table1.flyingHours} billion. Higher fuel prices are ${table1.fuel} billion. Lost equipment that CBO thinks the Pentagon would have to change its plans to replace is ${table1.equipment} billion. Other operations are ${table1.otherOps} billion. CBO could not estimate the cost of repairing damaged bases.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$21.7 billion: $7.3 billion for the land-attack cruise" /></li>
          <li>CBO projects that, because of higher energy prices, year-over-year PCE inflation in the first quarter of 2027 will be {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} percentage points higher than the agency's February 2026 forecast. PCE is the price index for personal consumption expenditures, the bundle of goods and services households buy. The letter says the Pentagon did not answer CBO's requests for information.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="0.5 percentage points higher" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="DoD did not respond to CBO’s requests for information" /></li>
        </ol>
      </aside>
      <p class="railed rail-note" data-layer="fact">Story updated {event.updated}.</p>

      <div data-layer="proof" class="event-record-context">
        <p class="sources-line">This story rests on CBO letter 62756 of September 15, 2026, to Rep. Brendan Boyle; Boyle's March 5 request letter and his March 5, June 24, and September 15 statements; the White House's March 12 Operation Epic Fury release; OMB Director Russell Vought's June 24 supplemental request; the Lead Inspector General report on Operation Epic Fury covering April 1 to June 30, 2026; and coverage checked against those records. CBO's site does not let its pages be saved by an ordinary fetch. The letter PDF and the publication page used here are Wayback Machine snapshots from September 17 and September 16.</p>
      </div>

      <section id="what-happened">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The letter, the clocks, and what is in the $38.1 billion</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On September 15, 2026, CBO Director Phillip Swagel signed a letter to Rep. Brendan Boyle, the top Democrat on the House Budget Committee, titled "Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran." Boyle had asked for the estimate on March 5, {computed.daysMar5ToSep15} days earlier, after the United States and Israel began strikes on February 28 under the name Operation Epic Fury. The letter also copies a list of Senate Democrats, including Warren, Merkley, and Schumer, who had asked CBO on May 28 for a true-cost estimate of the war.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran" /><Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="February 28, 2026" /><Cite s="warren-cbo-request-2026-05-28" passage="true cost of war in Iran" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO's Table 1 prints a total of ${table1.total} billion in 2026 dollars for DoD costs of Operation Epic Fury through August 1. The first-page summary of the same letter says the conflict has cost DoD approximately $38 billion. Both figures are CBO's. The table's five printed categories add to ${computed.table1RoundedSum} billion before CBO's rounding note.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Swagel opened with the number the summary uses.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Phillip Swagel, CBO director" when="September 15, 2026" where="Letter to Rep. Brendan Boyle">
          <p>approximately $38 billion<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">That total is a DoD incremental cost. CBO grouped it into replacing expended munitions, replacing equipment lost in battle, extra flying hours above peacetime averages, other operations for ships and ground units, and higher fuel prices. It does not include the basic operating costs of the forces already in the budget, costs to other agencies such as Postal Service fuel, future veterans' health care and disability, or a dollar figure for repairing U.S. bases.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="expended munitions and equipment lost in battle" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Munitions are the largest piece: ${munitions.total} billion, which is {computed.munitionsSharePct} percent of the Table 1 total. Of that, ${munitions.interceptors} billion is missile-defense interceptors (Patriot, THAAD, SM-3, and SM-6), ${munitions.cruise} billion is land-attack cruise missiles (Tomahawks and JASSM), and ${munitions.other} billion is other munitions. Extra flying hours are ${table1.flyingHours} billion: ${flyingHours.airForceTactical} billion for Air Force tactical aircraft, ${flyingHours.bombersSupport} billion for Air Force bombers and support aircraft, and ${flyingHours.navyTactical} billion for Navy tactical aircraft. Fuel is ${table1.fuel} billion. Equipment CBO thinks DoD would have to change its plans to replace is ${table1.equipment} billion; an alternative that priced every listed aircraft as if it needed new-buy money is ${battleLosses.alternativeBillion} billion. Table 2 prices four lost F-15Es at zero in CBO's column because an F-15EX buy is already in the budget. Other operations are ${table1.otherOps} billion, including hostile fire pay, combat-zone tax exclusions, and related special pays.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$21.7 billion: $7.3 billion for the land-attack cruise" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$2.4 billion by Navy tactical aircraft" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="hostile fire pay, combat zone" /></p>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>CBO Table 1, DoD costs through August 1</strong><span>Billions of 2026 dollars. Bases are not estimated.</span></figcaption>
          <BarChart bars={costBars} unit="b" ariaLabel="CBO Table 1 DoD cost categories in billions of dollars" />
          <p class="chart-source">Munitions ${munitions.total} billion is the largest bar. The printed categories plus CBO's rounding note produce the ${table1.total} billion total.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$21.7 billion: $7.3 billion for the land-attack cruise" /></p>
          <p class="chart-vintage" data-layer="proof">CBO letter 62756, Table 1, recovered from an Internet Archive copy of the PDF saved September 17, 2026.</p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">If fighting stays as quiet as May and June, CBO says another month costs ${monthly.low} billion. If it looks like July, the month costs ${monthly.july} billion. Monthly costs could be higher still if the violence escalated further.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="an additional month of conflict would cost $2 billion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Boyle asked for four things: DoD's operational, logistical, and sustainment costs; extra costs such as diplomacy and foreign aid; opportunity costs, including moving a carrier away from Taiwan; and the effect on prices. CBO answered each as a separate analysis and warned that the four are "not directly comparable or summable."<Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="The operational, logistical, and sustainment costs of the war in Iran" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="not directly comparable or summable" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On prices, CBO says reduced oil and gas shipments through the Strait of Hormuz and disruptions in the Red Sea pushed energy prices up. The letter also prints a price-level change: by the first quarter of 2027 the PCE price index is {inflation.pceLevel2027q1Pct} percent higher than the February forecast. That is not the same number as the year-over-year inflation rate, which is {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} percentage points higher; core PCE, which leaves out food and energy, is {inflation.corePce2027q1Pp} points higher on both the level and the year-over-year rate. Energy prices added {inflation.q2EnergyAddPp} percentage points to the annualized PCE inflation rate in the second quarter of 2026, when that rate was {inflation.q2PcePct} percent. Three-month Treasury bills are nearly {inflation.tbill2026Pp} percentage points higher in 2026 than in that February forecast. CBO says U.S. natural-gas prices have not increased significantly, because of domestic production, even as partners paid more. The effects on revenues and spending over 2026 to 2036 are modest and roughly offsetting, and the letter says it does not fold in effects on real output.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="0.5 percentage points higher" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="the PCE price index by 0.6 percent" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="prices in the United States have not increased" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="added 2.3 percentage points" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO built the DoD cost from government databases and public reports. The letter says twice that DoD did not respond to CBO's requests for information, and that the estimates are therefore "subject to considerable uncertainty."<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="DoD did not respond to CBO’s requests for information" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Boyle's September 15 statement rounded the Table 1 total the other way.<Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Brendan Boyle, House Budget ranking member" when="September 15, 2026" where="House Budget Committee Democrats statement">
          <p>this nonpartisan CBO report makes clear that the war has also cost American taxpayers tens of billions of dollars and counting, while continuing to drive up costs<Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="tens of billions of dollars and counting" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The letter's own so-what is the cutoff and the method: a DoD incremental cost through August 1, with munitions first, bases left blank, and the Pentagon not answering CBO.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbo-62756-iran" kind="record" detail>
          <p>Table 1 categories, billions of 2026 dollars through August 1: munitions {table1.munitions}, equipment {table1.equipment}, flying hours {table1.flyingHours}, other operations {table1.otherOps}, fuel {table1.fuel}, bases not available, total {table1.total}. CBO notes that numbers may not add to the total because of rounding.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="cbo-62756-iran" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The United States commenced Operation Epic Fury on February 28, 2026, with air and cruise missile strikes against Iran, along with coordinated Israeli airstrikes. CBO says DoD used that name for the first phase and later stopped; the letter applies it to the whole conflict. About 250 tactical aircraft, two carrier strike groups, and an amphibious ready group took part in the first phase. The United States has enforced a blockade of Iranian commercial shipping. A ceasefire began April 8. On July 10, after Iranian attacks on tankers, the president declared the ceasefire over.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="The United States commenced Operation Epic Fury on February 28, 2026" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="this letter refers to the entire conflict as Operation Epic Fury" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Approximately 250 tactical aircraft" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="has enforced a blockade of Iranian commercial shipping" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="wh-epic-fury-2026-03-12" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The White House's March 12 release stated the campaign's aims as obliterating Iran's ballistic missile arsenal and production capacity, annihilating its navy, severing support for terrorist proxies, and ensuring Iran never acquires a nuclear weapon.<Cite s="wh-epic-fury-2026-03-12" passage="obliterate Iran’s ballistic missile arsenal and production capacity" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="the-catch">
        <aside class="the-catch" data-layer="fact">
          <p class="tt-label">The catch</p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <h3>Four clocks, not one $38 billion.</h3>
              CBO's Table 1 is ${table1.total} billion through August 1. The inspector general quotes a Department of War estimate of ${ig.total} billion as of June 29. CBO says Hegseth told the Senate Armed Services Committee on July 22 that costs through September 2026 would be ${admin.hegeseth} billion. Military Times, dated July 21, reported Hegseth told the Senate Appropriations Committee the war had cost ${admin.hegeseth} billion to date. CBO also says those Hegseth components "appear to be roughly consistent" with its own categories except munitions. The clocks still differ by date and by what they include.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$37.5 billion" /><Cite s="militarytimes-hegseth-2026-07-21" passage="$37.5 billion to date" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Those cost components appear to be roughly consistent with CBO’s" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="$33.4 billion as of June 29" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#where-this-sits">Where this sits</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>$38 and $38.1 are both CBO's.</h3>
              The summary says "approximately $38 billion." Table 1 and the comparison with the supplemental say ${table1.total} billion. The five printed categories add to ${computed.table1RoundedSum} billion. CBO's own footnote is that numbers may not add to totals because of rounding. Boyle's statement said "nearly $40 billion."<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /><Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>The $87.6 billion request is not the war's sunk cost.</h3>
              OMB asked for $87.6 billion on June 24. CBO says ${admin.dodRequest} billion of that was for DoD, and ${admin.directRelated} billion looks directly related to the conflict. The rest includes ${admin.agriculture} billion for Agriculture, classified programs CBO did not analyze, and items CBO says are not a direct result of the fighting, including $1 billion for Penn Station. CBO could not estimate diplomatic and foreign-aid costs Boyle asked about.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="a request for $87.6 billion in" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$42.3 billion" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#claim-check">Claim check</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>$2 billion a month or $3 billion, not one number.</h3>
              CBO's low month is ${monthly.low} billion (May and June intensity). A July-like month is ${monthly.july} billion. Reuters led with a $3 billion monthly rise. CBS's URL says $2 billion per month; the CBS body uses the range.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="an additional month of conflict would cost $2 billion" /><Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="rise by $3 billion a month" />
              <a class="catch-jump" href="#outlets">Coverage</a>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="where-this-sits">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">How the cost clocks line up</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Where this sits</h2>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>From the first strikes to the CBO letter</strong><span>Dated steps from the White House release, Boyle's letter, the OMB request, CBO, and the inspector general.</span></figcaption>
          <DecisionTimeline entries={timeline} />
          <p class="chart-source">CBO's cost cutoff is August 1, {computed.daysFeb28ToAug1} days after February 28. The letter is dated September 15.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" /></p>
        </figure>
        <figure class="story-figure" data-layer="fact">
          <figcaption><strong>The same war, five published totals</strong><span>Billions of dollars. Each row is a different date and a different scope.</span></figcaption>
          <DataTable columns={["Clock", "Amount", "What it counts"]} rows={clocksRows} highlightColumn={1} />
          <p class="chart-source">CBO's ${table1.total} billion is {computed.daysJun29ToAug1} days later than the inspector general's ${ig.total} billion and is ${computed.cboMinusIg} billion higher. The ${admin.directRelated} billion "direct" slice of the June request is ${computed.directMinusCbo} billion above CBO's Table 1.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="$33.4 billion as of June 29" /></p>
        </figure>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The inspector general's first quarterly report on Operation Epic Fury, covering April 1 to June 30 and released September 9, quotes a Department of War estimate of ${ig.total} billion as of June 29: ${ig.obligations} billion of incremental obligations, ${ig.munitions} billion for expended munitions, and ${ig.equipment} billion in equipment losses, and does not include infrastructure repairs.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="$33.4 billion as of June 29" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="but does not include costs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO puts Hegseth's July 22 Senate testimony at ${admin.hegeseth} billion "through September 2026." Two weeks later, CBO says, DoD told committee staff the munitions piece had moved from $25 billion to $27.7 billion. That is still not Table 1's August 1 total.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$37.5 billion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The Hill, citing a congressional aide, reported that defense officials told committees on August 13 that the cost had climbed to more than $42 billion. That figure is the aide's, not a public Pentagon table. CBO's ${admin.directRelated} billion "direct" reading of the June request sits next to it; they are not the same document.<Cite s="hill-pentagon-42b-2026-09-15" passage="more than $42 billion" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$42.3 billion" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Vought's June 24 letter asks for $87.6 billion. The enclosed table assigns $67,146,000,000 to Department of War military programs, which CBO rounds to ${admin.dodRequest} billion, including $21 billion for munitions and $17.3 billion for operational costs. CBO's Table 3 also lists ${admin.agriculture} billion for Agriculture, $2.0 billion for Homeland Security, and $3.4 billion for State in that request. CBO says the operational-cost line is the main gap with its own ${table1.flyingHours} plus ${table1.equipment} plus ${table1.otherOps} billion for flying hours, equipment, and other operations.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="$67.1 billion for the Department of War" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$17.3 billion for “operational costs.”" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$11.1 billion for the Department of Agriculture" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO's opportunity-cost section is not another dollar total. It says the United States has "probably used between one-half and two-thirds of its inventory" of missile-defense interceptors since June 2025, counting the Twelve-Day War with Iran that year plus this campaign, and that rebuilding "would probably take at least five years" even if production rose. The letter names China and Taiwan as the stress case.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="between one-half and two-thirds" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="at least five years" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Those records add up to a scorekeeper's incremental DoD bill through August 1, sitting beside a June 29 department estimate, a July testimony CBO dates through September, and a June request that is larger because it buys more than the fighting already done.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /></p>
        <SourcedBlock source="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" kind="record" detail>
          <p>OMB Director Russell T. Vought wrote Speaker Mike Johnson on June 24 transmitting "a request for $87.6 billion in supplemental funding," most of it for Operation Epic Fury plus other needs including Ebola response and "hardworking American farmers."<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="a request for $87.6 billion in" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
        <SourcedBlock source="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" kind="record" detail>
          <p>The inspector general reports more than {ig.deployed.toLocaleString("en-US")} U.S. service members deployed in the Central Command area in support of the operation, and seven U.S. service members killed from February 28 to June 30.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="More than 50,000 U.S. Service members were deployed" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="seven U.S. Service members were killed" /></p>
        </SourcedBlock>
      </section>

      <section id="who-feels-it">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Prices at home, stockpiles, and the people already in the fight</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Who feels it</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO's inflation path is a household path. Brent crude, in CBO's telling, went from ${inflation.brentQ4_2025} a barrel in the fourth quarter of 2025 to a peak of ${inflation.brentQ2_2026} in the second quarter of 2026. The agency's updated path, using information through August 17, has Brent at ${inflation.brentEnd2026} by the end of 2026 and ${inflation.brentEnd2027} by the end of 2027, still {inflation.brentThisYearPctHigher} percent higher this year than the February forecast. Gasoline, diesel, and jet fuel moved first. By early 2027, CBO says, roughly 40 percent of the PCE-level change from the war is gasoline and other fuels; the rest is other goods and services as energy works through supply chains.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Brent crude prices rose from $64 a barrel" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="roughly 40 percent of the total change" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">That is why CBO raises year-over-year PCE inflation by {inflation.pce2027q1Pp} percentage points in the first quarter of 2027 against its own February baseline, and core PCE by {inflation.corePce2027q1Pp} points. Three-month Treasury bills are nearly {inflation.tbill2026Pp} points higher in 2026. CBO does not publish a mortgage rate or a pump price in this letter. NBC's $4.32 gasoline and 7.22 percent 30-year mortgage are NBC's, dated September 15, from Mortgage News Daily and pump data, not from Table 1.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="0.5 percentage points higher" /><Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="The average price of a gallon of gasoline in the U.S. is up 45%" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Inside the Pentagon, the fuel surcharge is already a bill. CBO says the Defense Logistics Agency added $42 a barrel from April to June, ${fuel.surchargeMillion} million extra for the services, and expected a ${fuel.dlaLossMillion} million loss on fuel sales in fiscal year 2026, together ${table1.fuel} billion.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="imposed a surcharge of $42 per barrel" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The interceptor drawdown is a later bill for whoever next needs those magazines. CBO prices Patriots and SM-6 interceptors at roughly $4 million each, THAAD at about $12 million, and the most sophisticated SM-3 at $28 million, and says production has historically lagged new orders by three to five years. The letter's China-and-Taiwan sentence is the reason it treats that drawdown as the main opportunity cost rather than as another line in Table 1.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="ranging from roughly $4 million" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="between one-half and two-thirds" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">On the ground, the inspector general counts more than {ig.deployed.toLocaleString("en-US")} U.S. service members in the Central Command area for this operation through June 30, and seven killed between February 28 and June 30. State told the inspectors that damage to U.S. embassies and diplomatic facilities in Iraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, and the United Arab Emirates was about ${ig.diplomaticMillion} million as of June 30, and that it had incurred ${ig.stateTotalMillion} million related to the conflict as of June 2, including ${ig.stateContingencyMillion} million for evacuations and related contingencies. CBO still has no dollar figure for repairing U.S. military bases, because DoD did not provide values and host nations might pay part of any rebuild.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="seven U.S. Service members were killed" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="approximately $184 million" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Farmers sit in the June request, not in Table 1. OMB asked for ${admin.agriculture} billion for Agriculture, including $10 billion of temporary economic assistance for 2026 row and specialty crops, which CBO links to higher fuel and fertilizer prices after the Strait of Hormuz closed to commercial shipping. That money has not been enacted.<Cite s="omb-supplemental-2026-06-24" passage="$11.1 billion to support" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$11.1 billion for the Department of Agriculture" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">CBO does not assign a dollar to the Americans already dead or wounded. The letter says so. Boyle's March 5 statement had already named "the tragic deaths of American service members." The inspector general's seven through June 30 is the official count this page can cite; Reuters later wrote that eighteen have died.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="does not assign financial costs to U.S. military personnel" /><Cite s="boyle-cbo-request-2026-03-05" passage="the tragic deaths of American service members" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="seven U.S. Service members were killed" /><Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="Eighteen U.S. military service members have died in the Iran war" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">What the records add up to for a reader is a war bill that has already shown up in energy prices and interceptor magazines, a Pentagon incremental cost CBO can count through August 1, and a set of people (troops, diplomats, farmers) whose later bills CBO either cannot price or parked in a request Congress has not passed.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="outlets">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Nine outlets on one letter</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2>
        <p class="oc-count" data-layer="fact">Nine outlets checked against CBO letter 62756. Errors first.</p>
        <div data-layer="fact">
        <OutletCheck outlet="Reuters" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote that the amount "is projected to rise by $3 billion a month" and that Hegseth reported $37.5 billion "at a Senate hearing on July 21."`} verdict="CBO's monthly figure is $2 billion in a quiet month and $3 billion in a July-like month, not a single $3 billion path. CBO dates Hegseth July 22 before the Senate Armed Services Committee. Military Times, dated July 21, reported the same $37.5 billion as to date at a Senate Appropriations hearing. Reuters's date matches that hearing coverage, not CBO's later letter.">
          <Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="rise by $3 billion a month" />
          <Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="On July 22, 2026, in testimony about that request" />
          <Cite s="militarytimes-hegseth-2026-07-21" passage="Senate Appropriations Committee" />
        </OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheck outlet="ABC News" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote that the war has cost "at least $38 billion through the end of July."`} verdict="CBO's cutoff is August 1, not the end of July. The summary says approximately $38 billion; Table 1 says $38.1 billion.">
          <Cite s="abc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="through the end of July" />
          <Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" />
        </OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheck outlet="NBC News" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote that CBO "specifically blames the war for more than 40% of inflation during the second quarter of 2026."`} verdict="CBO says higher energy prices added 2.3 percentage points to annualized PCE inflation in that quarter, when PCE inflation was 5.3 percent. 2.3 divided by 5.3 is 43.4 percent. CBO did not print a 40 percent share. Senator Warren's September 15 statement used the same more-than-40-percent rounding.">
          <Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than 40% of inflation during the second quarter of 2026" />
          <Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="added 2.3 percentage points" />
          <Cite s="warren-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="more than 40% of inflation from the second quarter of 2026" />
        </OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheck outlet="House Budget Committee Democrats" status="warn" chip="mislabeled" claim={`wrote that the war "directly cost the United States nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026."`} verdict="CBO's Table 1 is $38.1 billion for DoD incremental costs. Nearly $40 billion is Boyle's rounding, not CBO's printed total.">
          <Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="directly cost the United States nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" />
        </OutletCheck>
        <OutletCheckFold count={5} method="the CBO letter's Table 1 total, munitions split, monthly range, and inflation sentences.">
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="Associated Press" status="ok" chip="checks out" claim={`wrote "more than $38 billion through Aug. 1" and "up to $3 billion a month or more depending on the intensity."`} verdict="Checks out. More than $38 billion matches Table 1's $38.1 billion. The monthly clause tracks CBO's $2 billion / $3 billion / higher-still range.">
            <Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than $38 billion through Aug. 1" />
          </OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="Politico" status="ok" chip="checks out" claim={`wrote CBO's "$38.1 billion estimate" and the $21.7 / $13.1 / $7.3 / $1.2 billion munitions split.`} verdict="Checks out against the letter. 'First five months' is Politico's label for February 28 through August 1.">
            <Cite s="politico-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="$38.1 billion estimate for the war" />
          </OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="CNBC" status="ok" chip="checks out" claim={`wrote "$38.1 billion through Aug. 1" and a derived "$246 million per day."`} verdict="Checks out on the total. $38.1 billion over 155 days from February 28 through August 1 inclusive is $245.8 million a day, which rounds to CNBC's $246 million.">
            <Cite s="cnbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="estimated $38.1 billion through Aug. 1" />
          </OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="CBS News" status="ok" chip="checks out" claim={`the body wrote "$2 billion to $3 billion per month" and "about $38 billion" from February 28 through August 1.`} verdict="Checks out in the body. The URL and headline lean on the $2 billion low end of the same range.">
            <Cite s="cbs-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="$2 billion to $3 billion per month" />
          </OutletCheck>
          <OutletCheck folded outlet="The Hill" status="ok" chip="checks out" claim={`wrote "around $38 billion as of Aug. 1" for CBO and, separately, that the Pentagon told Congress on August 13 the cost was "over $42 billion."`} verdict="The CBO figure checks out. The $42 billion August 13 update is The Hill citing a congressional aide, not CBO, and is a single-outlet public account of a closed briefing.">
            <Cite s="hill-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="around $38 billion as of Aug. 1 this year" />
          </OutletCheck>
        </OutletCheckFold>
        </div>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The White House answer in the coverage is Anna Kelly's, as NBC and Reuters printed it, not a CBO finding.<Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles" /></p>
        <QuoteCard speaker="Anna Kelly, White House spokeswoman" when="September 15, 2026" where="As quoted by NBC News">
          <p>The United States Military has more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles to serve all of the Commander-in-Chief’s strategic goals and beyond.<Cite s="nbc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="more than enough munitions, ammo, and stockpiles" /></p>
        </QuoteCard>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Reuters also quoted chief Pentagon spokesman Sean Parnell: "We have everything required to strike at the time and place of the president’s choosing." The Pentagon's own page for that sentence is not among the records here.<Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="We have everything required to strike at the time and place of the president’s choosing" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">What the coverage adds up to: the $38.1 billion through August 1 and the munitions split were widely right. The monthly path, Hegseth's date, the end-of-July cutoff, a 40 percent inflation share, and Boyle's nearly $40 billion were not.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /><Cite s="boyle-cbo-findings-2026-09-15" passage="nearly $40 billion through August 1, 2026" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="claim-check">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The $38 billion readers arrived with</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2>
        <div data-layer="fact">
          <CheckedBlock label="The Iran war has cost $38 billion" variant="confirmed">
            <p>CBO's own summary says the conflict has cost DoD "approximately $38 billion" as of August 1. Table 1 prints ${table1.total} billion in 2026 dollars. That is a DoD incremental cost: munitions, extra flying hours, fuel, some lost equipment, and other operations. It is not a full-government cost, not a bill for damaged bases, not veterans' care, and not the ${admin.supplementalTotal} billion June request.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="approximately $38 billion" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Comparing CBO’s estimate ($38.1 billion) with" /></p>
            <p>The inspector general's ${ig.total} billion as of June 29 is the department's own earlier clock, {computed.daysJun29ToAug1} days before CBO's cutoff, and excludes infrastructure. Hegseth's ${admin.hegeseth} billion, in CBO's account of the July 22 testimony, is "through September 2026," a different end date.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="$33.4 billion as of June 29" /><Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="$37.5 billion" /></p>
          </CheckedBlock>
        </div>
        <details class="receipt" data-layer="proof"><summary>how the shares and day counts were computed</summary>
          <p>Munitions share: {munitions.total} / {table1.total} = {computed.munitionsSharePct} percent. Energy share of Q2 PCE inflation, used only to check NBC: {inflation.q2EnergyAddPp} / {inflation.q2PcePct} = {computed.q2EnergySharePct} percent. Days from February 28 to August 1: {computed.daysFeb28ToAug1} elapsed days. Inclusive days February 28 through August 1: 155. {table1.total} billion / 155 = ${computed.perDayInclusiveMillion} million a day. Table 1 printed parts {table1.munitions} + {table1.equipment} + {table1.flyingHours} + {table1.otherOps} + {table1.fuel} = {computed.table1RoundedSum}, against a printed total of {table1.total}. Direct-request minus CBO: {admin.directRelated} - {table1.total} = {computed.directMinusCbo}. CBO minus IG: {table1.total} - {ig.total} = {computed.cboMinusIg}.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

      <section id="what-happened-next">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The same Tuesday, and the request still sitting</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What happened next</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">AP reported on September 15 that Congress has not approved the June request. ABC quoted Senate Majority Leader John Thune that day saying the funding push is not before the election.<Cite s="ap-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="but Congress has not approved it" /><Cite s="abc-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="Well, not before the election" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The inspector general's quarterly report had landed September 9, six days before the CBO letter, with the ${ig.total} billion June 29 department estimate and the "strategic inventory shortfalls" line outlets paired with CBO's interceptor section.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="strategic inventory shortfalls" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Watching: whether Congress enacts any of the June request after November 3; whether CBO updates Table 1 past August 1; whether DoD answers the next information request; and the next inspector general quarterly after June 30.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="unknowns">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions the letter itself flags</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The cost of repairing or rebuilding U.S. bases. CBO says it cannot estimate that cost because DoD did not provide values and host nations might pay part of a rebuild. The inspector general likewise excludes infrastructure from the ${ig.total} billion.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO is unable to estimate the cost" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="but does not include costs" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Diplomatic operations and foreign aid, which Boyle asked about. CBO "was unable to estimate" those costs. State's ${ig.stateTotalMillion} million through June 2 in the inspector general report is State's own figure for that period, not CBO's missing category filled in.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="CBO was unable to estimate potential" /><Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="incurred $113 million in costs related to the" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">A ground-force scenario. Boyle asked CBO to consider the war lasting longer than four to five weeks and deploying U.S. troops on the ground. The September 15 letter does not publish a separate ground-deployment cost case.<Cite s="boyle-letter-to-cbo-2026-03-05" passage="deploying U.S. troops on the ground in Iran" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The August 13 "more than $42 billion" Pentagon briefing The Hill reported from an aide. No public table from that briefing is in this story's records. CBO's ${admin.directRelated} billion is a reading of the June request, not that briefing.<Cite s="hill-pentagon-42b-2026-09-15" passage="Defense officials provided the updated cost estimate to congressional defense committees on Aug. 13" /></p>
        <p data-layer="narrative">Deaths after June 30. The inspector general counts seven U.S. service members killed through June 30. Reuters on September 15 wrote that eighteen have died. No later official casualty table is among the records here.<Cite s="dod-ig-oef-q3-2026-09-09" passage="seven U.S. Service members were killed" /><Cite s="reuters-cbo-iran-2026-09-15" passage="Eighteen U.S. military service members have died in the Iran war" /></p>
      </section>

      <section id="records">
        <p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">What this page is built from</p>
        <h2 data-layer="fact">The records</h2>
        <p data-layer="narrative">The primary is CBO letter 62756. Boyle's request, the White House campaign release, the OMB supplemental letter, and the inspector general quarterly sit with it. Coverage is checked against those, not the other way around.<Cite s="cbo-62756-iran" passage="Estimating the Cost of Combat Operations Against Iran" /></p>
      </section>

